insert into public.scripture_did_you_know(reference,fact,source_note) values
('Genesis 15:1','Before Abram hears about descendants, God first calls Himself Abram''s shield. The promise begins with protection, not a number.','Genesis 15:1.'),
('Genesis 15:6','Genesis says Abram believed the Lord, and that faith was counted to him as righteousness. Paul later returns to this exact verse when explaining faith.','Genesis 15:6; Romans 4:3.'),
('Genesis 28:12','In Jacob''s dream, the angels are described as ascending and descending. The verse names their movement in that order: going up first, then down.','Genesis 28:12.'),
('Genesis 28:19','Jacob called the place Bethel, meaning ''house of God,'' but the chapter says the city''s earlier name was Luz.','Genesis 28:17–19; Hebrew Bet-El.'),
('Exodus 3:2','Moses saw flames in a bush without the bush burning up. His decision to turn aside and look more closely is written into the story.','Exodus 3:2–4.'),
('Exodus 3:5','Moses is told to remove his sandals because the ground is holy. The place becomes significant because of God''s presence, not because it was a famous building.','Exodus 3:5.'),
('Exodus 12:3','The Passover lamb was chosen on the tenth day of the month, but kept until the fourteenth. The instructions make preparation part of the celebration.','Exodus 12:3–6.'),
('Exodus 12:46','Israel was told not to break a bone of the Passover lamb. John''s Gospel later draws attention to Jesus'' unbroken bones in the crucifixion account.','Exodus 12:46; John 19:33–36.'),
('Leviticus 16:8','On the Day of Atonement, two goats were presented and lots were cast over them. Their different roles are described in the chapter.','Leviticus 16:7–10.'),
('Leviticus 16:21','The high priest placed both hands on the living goat and confessed Israel''s wrongs before it was sent away. The ritual used a visible picture of removal.','Leviticus 16:20–22.'),
('Numbers 21:8','When the people were bitten by serpents, Moses was told to raise a bronze serpent so they could look and live. Jesus later referred to that scene when speaking about being lifted up.','Numbers 21:8–9; John 3:14–15.'),
('Numbers 21:17','Numbers records a song addressed to a well: ''Spring up, O well!'' Even in the middle of a travel account, Israel''s journey includes communal singing.','Numbers 21:17–18.'),
('Deuteronomy 6:7','Israel was told to talk about God''s commands while sitting at home, travelling, lying down, and getting up. Learning was meant to belong to everyday life.','Deuteronomy 6:6–7.'),
('Deuteronomy 6:9','The commandments were to be written on doorposts and gates. The instruction connects worship with the places people entered and left every day.','Deuteronomy 6:8–9.'),
('Joshua 2:15','Rahab''s house was built into the city wall, which explains how she could let the spies down by a rope from her window.','Joshua 2:15.'),
('Joshua 2:18','The spies asked Rahab to tie a scarlet cord in her window. The marker mattered because her household was being promised protection.','Joshua 2:17–21.'),
('Joshua 6:3','For six days the people were to circle Jericho once per day. On the seventh day the number of circuits changed to seven.','Joshua 6:3–4.'),
('Joshua 6:20','The priests blew their trumpets and the people shouted before the wall fell. The story links their obedience to the unusual instructions they had received.','Joshua 6:4–5,20.'),
('Judges 4:5','Deborah gave judgments beneath a palm tree between Ramah and Bethel. Israel''s judge was pictured at an accessible meeting place, not in a palace.','Judges 4:4–5.'),
('Judges 4:21','The final blow against Sisera came from Jael using a tent peg. The unlikely household object changes the course of this battle narrative.','Judges 4:17–22.'),
('Ruth 1:16','Ruth''s promise includes both Naomi''s people and Naomi''s God. She does not simply agree to travel; she accepts a new belonging.','Ruth 1:16–17.'),
('Ruth 1:22','Naomi and Ruth arrived in Bethlehem at the beginning of barley harvest. That small time detail prepares the story for Ruth''s encounter in the fields.','Ruth 1:22; 2:2–3.'),
('1 Samuel 3:7','Samuel heard his name before he recognized who was speaking. The account says he did not yet know the Lord in this way.','1 Samuel 3:4–8.'),
('1 Samuel 3:19','The narrator says none of Samuel''s words fell to the ground. It is a vivid way of describing the reliability of his prophetic ministry.','1 Samuel 3:19–20.'),
('2 Samuel 7:6','When David planned to build a house for God, the Lord reminded him that He had travelled with Israel in a tent. The response starts by changing the king''s assumptions.','2 Samuel 7:5–7.'),
('2 Samuel 7:12','David wanted to build God a house, but God promised to establish David''s house—that is, his royal line. The chapter turns one meaning of ''house'' into another.','2 Samuel 7:11–16.'),
('1 Kings 3:9','Solomon asked for an understanding heart to judge the people, not for longer life or wealth. The request surprised even within the biblical narrative.','1 Kings 3:9–13.'),
('1 Kings 3:26','The true mother revealed herself not by a speech about identity, but by begging that the living baby should be spared. Her compassion became the decisive clue.','1 Kings 3:24–27.'),
('2 Kings 5:2','A young Israelite girl taken captive became the person who first pointed Naaman''s household toward the prophet in Samaria.','2 Kings 5:2–3.'),
('2 Kings 5:10','Elisha told Naaman to wash seven times in the Jordan. Naaman almost missed his healing because the instruction seemed too ordinary.','2 Kings 5:10–14.'),
('Esther 4:14','Mordecai never claims to know exactly how Esther reached the palace. His challenge is a question: who knows whether she came to royalty for such a time?','Esther 4:14.'),
('Esther 4:16','Esther asked others to fast for three days before she approached the king. She also said she and her attendants would fast.','Esther 4:16.'),
('Job 1:5','Job offered sacrifices for his children because he feared they might have sinned inwardly. His concern reached beyond what he could see.','Job 1:4–5.'),
('Job 1:21','Job responds to loss by remembering he entered the world with nothing and would leave with nothing. His declaration comes before the book''s long debates begin.','Job 1:20–22.'),
('Psalm 23:4','The psalm changes from talking about God as ''He'' to speaking directly to God as ''You'' in the darkest valley. The grammar becomes personal at the moment of fear.','Psalm 23:1–4; compare third-person and second-person address.'),
('Psalm 23:5','After the picture of a shepherd comes the picture of a host preparing a table. Both images describe care, even in the presence of enemies.','Psalm 23:1–5.'),
('Proverbs 3:5','The proverb tells readers to trust the Lord with all their heart, then warns them not to lean only on their own understanding. Trust and humility appear side by side.','Proverbs 3:5–6.'),
('Proverbs 3:11','The same chapter that promises guidance also speaks about correction from the Lord. Wisdom is not presented as a life without discipline.','Proverbs 3:11–12.'),
('Isaiah 6:2','Isaiah sees seraphim with six wings each: two cover the face, two cover the feet, and two are used for flight.','Isaiah 6:2.'),
('Isaiah 6:6','The coal that touches Isaiah''s mouth is taken from the altar. The scene connects cleansing with worship before Isaiah is sent with a message.','Isaiah 6:5–8.'),
('Isaiah 9:2','The prophecy speaks of light appearing to people walking in deep darkness. Matthew later quotes this passage when describing the start of Jesus'' ministry in Galilee.','Isaiah 9:1–2; Matthew 4:13–16.'),
('Isaiah 9:6','The promised child is described using royal titles, including ''Prince of Peace.'' The prophecy speaks about rulership as well as birth.','Isaiah 9:6–7.'),
('Isaiah 53:7','The servant is compared to a lamb led to slaughter and remains silent before oppressors. Acts later shows an Ethiopian official reading this very passage.','Isaiah 53:7–8; Acts 8:32–35.'),
('Isaiah 53:5','Isaiah links the servant''s wounds with healing and the punishment with peace. Christians read this pattern through the death of Jesus.','Isaiah 53:4–6; 1 Peter 2:24.'),
('Jeremiah 29:7','Jeremiah told exiles to seek the welfare of the city where they were living. The surprising instruction was given while they were far from home.','Jeremiah 29:4–7.'),
('Jeremiah 29:10','The promise of return is set after seventy years for Babylon. Jeremiah 29:11 comes inside a letter about waiting, faithfulness, and restoration—not immediate escape.','Jeremiah 29:10–14.'),
('Ezekiel 37:7','In Ezekiel''s vision the bones come together before breath enters them. There are two distinct movements in the description.','Ezekiel 37:7–10.'),
('Ezekiel 37:11','God tells Ezekiel what the dry bones represent: the whole house of Israel saying its hope is lost. The primary vision concerns a people in exile.','Ezekiel 37:11–14.'),
('Daniel 6:10','Daniel prayed three times a day facing Jerusalem, just as he had done before the decree was signed. The account stresses that his practice did not suddenly begin in crisis.','Daniel 6:10.'),
('Daniel 6:22','Daniel says God sent His angel and shut the lions'' mouths. The text specifically attributes his deliverance to God rather than Daniel''s cleverness.','Daniel 6:21–23.'),
('Jonah 3:6','When Nineveh''s king heard the warning, he rose from his throne, laid aside his robe, and put on sackcloth. The response reached the highest office.','Jonah 3:6–9.'),
('Jonah 3:10','The chapter ends by saying God saw what the people did when they turned from evil. Their changed actions, not merely their words, are explicitly mentioned.','Jonah 3:10.'),
('Matthew 5:14','Jesus compared His disciples to a city set on a hill that cannot be hidden. The image presents influence as something publicly visible.','Matthew 5:14–16.'),
('Matthew 5:44','Jesus'' command to love enemies comes after He contrasts what people had heard with what He is now teaching. His Kingdom ethic reaches beyond ordinary reciprocity.','Matthew 5:43–48.'),
('Luke 2:8','The first public announcement in Luke''s birth story comes to shepherds out in the fields at night, not to officials in a royal palace.','Luke 2:8–12.'),
('Luke 2:21','Luke records that Jesus was named on the eighth day, the time of circumcision. The name had already been given by the angel before His birth.','Luke 1:31; 2:21.'),
('John 3:3','The Greek word anōthen in Jesus'' conversation with Nicodemus can mean both ''again'' and ''from above.'' The exchange plays on the depth of that expression.','John 3:3–7; Greek ἄνωθεν.'),
('John 3:14','Jesus refers to Moses lifting up the serpent in the wilderness when speaking of the Son of Man being lifted up. One rescue image points toward another.','John 3:14–15; Numbers 21:8–9.'),
('Acts 2:11','The crowd heard the followers of Jesus speaking about God''s works in their own languages. Acts lists many lands represented among the listeners.','Acts 2:5–11.'),
('Acts 2:41','Acts says about three thousand people were added after Peter''s message. The same chapter goes on to describe what that new community did together.','Acts 2:41–47.'),
('Romans 8:15','Paul preserves the Aramaic word Abba and immediately explains it with the Greek word for father. His picture of adoption includes the confidence to address God personally.','Romans 8:15; Galatians 4:6; Aramaic abba.'),
('Romans 8:26','Paul says the Spirit helps believers in weakness when they do not know how to pray. The chapter does not assume mature believers always have the right words.','Romans 8:26–27.'),
('Ephesians 2:8','Grace is the gift and faith is the means of receiving it; Paul says salvation is not something earned by works. The next verse leads straight into a new life of good works.','Ephesians 2:8–10.'),
('Ephesians 2:19','Paul calls believers fellow citizens and members of God''s household. He uses both political and family language to describe belonging to God''s Kingdom.','Ephesians 2:19–22.'),
('Revelation 21:12','The city in John''s vision has twelve gates bearing the names of Israel''s twelve tribes. The city''s design deliberately remembers covenant history.','Revelation 21:12–13.'),
('Revelation 21:23','John says the city has no need for the sun or moon to shine on it because God''s glory lights it and the Lamb is its lamp. This is visionary imagery, not an archaeological description.','Revelation 21:22–24.')
